Scalers and float LUT textures on GL 2.0 / GLSL 1.20. Two parts:

=== 1 ===
opengl/formats: add GL_ARB_texture_float 16-bit and luminance formats on GL 2.0

Without GL_ARB_texture_rg the GL 2.0 path only registered rgb32f and
rgba32f as float texture formats. Every float LUT with one or two
components therefore had no texture to live in: sh_lut() fell through
to "Can't generate LUT: no compatible methods!" for the 64x64 blue-noise
dither matrix (too large for uniforms), so mpv's default dither silently
degraded to white noise, and the 1-component polar scaler LUT had no
texture either.

GL_ARB_texture_float, which these drivers do advertise, also defines
GL_LUMINANCE(_ALPHA){16,32}F_ARB, so register those as sampleable-only
formats next to rgb16f/rgba16f (uploaded from 32-bit floats like
upstream's formats_float16). The 32-bit luminance variants come first so
LUTs that only need nearest sampling get full precision; they carry no
linear flag since the GPUs of that era (NV4x/G7x, R5xx) filter 16-bit
floats at most. None of them are renderable, matching the existing
rgb(a)32f entries.

On a GeForce 6600 (GL 2.0 NVIDIA-1.5.48) this lets the dither LUT use a
luminance32f texture through the texelFetch() emulation.

=== 2 ===
shaders/sampling: run the scalers on GLSL < 130

The bicubic, hermite, gaussian, oversample, polar and separated (ortho)
samplers and the bicubic distortion mode were disabled on GLSL < 130
because they query the source size with textureSize(). On a GL 2.x
context mpv's default lanczos chroma and luma scaling therefore fell
back to the sampler's bilinear filter ("Failed dispatching scaler..
disabling").

The texture dimensions are known when the shader is generated, so on
GLSL < 130 pass them in as a vec2 uniform (sh_tex_size) instead; GLSL
>= 130 keeps textureSize(). On top of that:

- ortho: the convolution loop uses uint, integer modulo and dynamic
  vector indexing, none of which GLSL 1.20 has, and the GPUs of that era
  cannot loop at run time in fragment shaders anyway. On GLSL < 130 emit
  the loop unrolled from C with constant .xyzw indices; the GLSL >= 130
  loop is unchanged.
- polar: the fragment path only needs the size and a 1-component linear
  LUT (luminance16f on GL 2.0 without GL_ARB_texture_rg); wrap the
  antiring mix() selector in float() like the other GLSL 1.20 fixes.
- oversample: wrap the lessThan()/greaterThan() mix() selectors in vec2().
- distort: drop the GLSL < 130 bicubic override.

Shader length is the remaining limit: NV4x/G7x handle the unrolled
lanczos kernels, while R3xx/R4xx fragment programs (64 ALU / 32 TEX
instructions) may still fail to compile larger kernels, in which case
the renderer falls back to bilinear exactly as before.

